>
  <p>Zaaheen is an independent AI consultancy based in Dubai that assesses businesses across the
     GCC on site, team by team, and tells each one whether it needs AI, plain automation, a better
     process, or nothing at all. We sell and build no tools, so we have no reason to prefer one
     answer over another.</p>

  <section id="difference">
    <h2>What is the difference between AI and automation?</h2>
    <p>Automation follows rules you write down in advance; AI reads, writes and makes judgements
       about things that do not follow a fixed pattern.</p>
    <p>An automated workflow does exactly what it is told. When an invoice arrives, check the
       purchase order number, compare the amounts, and send it for payment if they match. It never
       gets tired, and it never guesses. It also cannot cope with anything it was not told about.</p>
    <p>An AI model works on language and patterns instead of rules. It can read an email written in
       any style, pull out what the customer wants, draft a reply, or sort a pile of documents by
       what they are about. It copes with variety, but it can be wrong in ways a rule never is, so
       its output needs checking.</p>
    <p>People often ask about RPA vs AI. RPA (robotic process automation) is a kind of automation:
       software that clicks through screens the way a person would, following fixed steps. It sits
       on the automation side of the line, not the AI side.</p>
    <table class="learn-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Automation</th><th scope="col">AI</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">How it works</th><td>Fixed rules, written in advance</td><td>Learned patterns, applied to new cases</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Best inputs</th><td>Clean, structured, the same every time</td><td>Varied: emails, documents, free text</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">When it goes wrong</th><td>Stops, or flags the exception</td><td>Can give a confident wrong answer</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Checking needed</th><td>Set up once, then spot checks</td><td>A person reviews where mistakes cost money</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Running cost</th><td>Usually low and predictable</td><td>Varies with use; needs ongoing review</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="automation">
    <h2>Which tasks only need automation?</h2>
    <p>If the rules never change and the information arrives clean, you do not need AI: a form, a
       workflow or a link between two systems does the job more cheaply and more safely.</p>
    <p>This answers a question many business owners in Dubai and Riyadh ask: do I need AI or
       just automation? For a surprising amount of office work, the honest answer is just
       automation. Here are common examples, with the verdict we would usually give.</p>
    <table class="learn-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Task</th><th scope="col">Fixed rules?</th><th scope="col">Clean inputs?</th><th scope="col">Usual verdict</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Matching supplier invoices to purchase orders</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes, if suppliers use the PO number</td><td>Automate without AI</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Sending payment reminders on day 30, 45 and 60</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Automate without AI</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Copying web enquiries into the sales system</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes, with a proper form</td><td>Automate without AI</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Approving leave against a balance</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Automate without AI</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Answering varied customer emails</th><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>AI now, with a person checking</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Approvals nobody clearly owns</th><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Fix the process first</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Negotiating a key contract</th><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Leave as it is</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The verdicts in the last column are the four we give in
       <a href="/consulting/">our AI Readiness Assessment</a>: AI now, Automate without AI, Fix the
       process first, and Leave as it is.</p>

