  <section id="ai">
    <h2>Which tasks really need AI?</h2>
    <p>AI earns its place when the work means reading, writing, sorting or answering at volume, the
       inputs vary every time, and the information it needs already exists.</p>
    <p>Typical examples from office teams in the UAE, Saudi Arabia and Qatar:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Customer service:</strong> sorting incoming emails and chat messages by what
          the customer needs, and drafting first replies for a person to approve.</li>
      <li><strong>Sales:</strong> reading a price request written in free text and pulling out the
          products, quantities and dates.</li>
      <li><strong>Finance:</strong> reading invoices that arrive as scanned documents in different
          layouts, where no two suppliers send the same format.</li>
      <li><strong>HR:</strong> answering the same policy questions from staff, from the handbook you
          already have.</li>
      <li><strong>Risk and compliance:</strong> a first read of long documents to flag the clauses a
          specialist should look at.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Notice what these have in common: the volume is high, the work repeats, and a person stays
       in charge of anything that matters. If you want to find tasks like these in your own
       business, our guide on <a href="/learn/where-ai-helps-your-business/">how to find where AI
       helps your business</a> walks through the method step by step.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="risk">
    <h2>Why is AI riskier than plain automation?</h2>
    <p>AI is riskier because it can be wrong without knowing it, and because projects that use it
       fail far more often than ordinary IT work.</p>
    <p>A rule either matches or it does not. AI gives an answer every time, and some of those
       answers will be wrong while looking right. That means someone has to check the output, and
       that checking time belongs in the cost.</p>
    <p>The wider record points the same way. RAND interviewed 65 data scientists and engineers and
       reported in August 2024 that, "by some estimates, more than 80 percent of AI projects fail,
       twice the rate of non-AI IT projects". The most common cause it found was that people
       misunderstood or miscommunicated what problem the AI was meant to solve
       (<a href={RAND}>RAND, 13 August 2024</a>).</p>

    <BarChart
      id="chart-ai-vs-automation"
      title="Three studies, one pattern: the problem is rarely the tool (per cent)"
      measure="Per cent"
      max={100}
      bars={[
        { label: 'AI projects that fail', value: 80, note: 'or more' },
        { label: 'No workflow redesign', value: 84 },
        { label: 'No value from AI yet', value: 74 },
      ]}
      source={{ text: 'RAND, 13 August 2024: "by some estimates, more than 80 percent of AI projects fail". Deloitte Middle East, 4 June 2026: 84% of organisations have not redesigned jobs or workflows around AI (3,200+ leaders, 24 countries). BCG, 24 October 2024: 74% of companies have yet to show tangible value from AI (1,000 executives, 59 countries).', href: RAND }}
    />

    <p>Deloitte's 2026 survey found that 84% of organisations had not yet redesigned jobs or
       workflows around AI (<a href={DELOITTE}>Deloitte Middle East, 4 June 2026</a>), and BCG found
       in 2024 that 74% of companies had yet to show tangible value from it
       (<a href={BCG}>BCG, 24 October 2024</a>). None of this says AI does not work. It says AI
       put on the wrong task, or on an unchanged process, usually does not pay. We look at that in
       more depth in <a href="/learn/why-ai-projects-fail/">why AI projects fail</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="process">
    <h2>What if the process itself is the problem?</h2>
    <p>If a process has too many exceptions, no clear owner or missing data, neither AI nor
       automation will fix it; AI on a broken process only makes it break faster.</p>
    <p>This is a verdict a seller of software has little reason to give. Yet it is
       one of the most common sights on a busy desk at month end: an approval that passes
       through three people because nobody knows who decides, a spreadsheet that only one person
       understands, or customer details typed again into a second system because the first one
       was never set up properly.</p>
    <p>Signs the process needs fixing before any technology:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Staff describe the same task in different ways.</li>
      <li>More than a handful of cases are "exceptions" that someone handles by hand.</li>
      <li>Nobody can say who owns a step when it goes wrong.</li>
      <li>The data a tool would need is incomplete, or kept in personal files.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Fixing these first is often cheap. Sometimes it removes the need for a tool altogether, and
       when it does not, the automation or AI you add afterwards has a fair chance of working. Before
       you spend, it also pays to <a href="/learn/ai-roi-before-you-buy/">work out the return
       before you buy</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="together">
    <h2>Can AI and automation work together?</h2>
    <p>Yes, and in many good set-ups they do: automation handles the fixed steps, AI handles the
       one step that needs reading or judgement, and a person checks what matters.</p>
    <p>Take a customer request arriving by email. AI reads it and works out what the customer
       wants. A plain workflow then creates the record, routes it to the right team and sends the
       acknowledgement. A person approves any reply that commits the business to a price or a
       date.</p>
    <p>This is also the honest answer to the question of AI agents vs workflow automation. An
       agent is AI deciding which steps to take; a workflow is steps you decided in advance. For
       most office work in the Gulf today, the safer design is a fixed workflow with AI used at the
       one or two points that need it, rather than an agent left to decide the whole route.</p>
  </section>

