  <section id="faq">
    <h2>What else do people ask about AI vs automation?</h2>

    <h3>Do I need AI or just automation?</h3>
    <p>Ask two questions about the task: are the rules fixed, and is the information clean? If both
       answers are yes, plain automation is usually cheaper and safer. If the work means reading or
       writing things that vary, AI may help, with a person checking the output.</p>

    <h3>Is RPA the same as AI?</h3>
    <p>No. RPA follows fixed steps on screen, the way a person would click through a system, so it
       is a form of automation. Some products now add AI to RPA, but the two do different jobs:
       rules on one side, reading and judgement on the other.</p>

    <h3>When should you not use AI?</h3>
    <p>When the volume is low, when an expert judgement carries a costly risk, or when the process
       is still unclear. In the first two cases the work should stay with your people; in the third,
       fix the process first. We say so in a report when it applies.</p>

    <h3>Do you sell or build the tools you recommend?</h3>
    <p>No. We do not sell, resell or build any AI product, software or hardware, and we recommend
